\section{Related studies}
This section provides a comprehensive overview of various learning approaches widely proposed for B5G networks, followed by recent state-of-the-art studies targeting traffic forecasting using any of the mentioned learning approaches. Finally, the related work for DU pooling strategies by academia and industry is discussed.

\subsection{Distributed learning approaches}
Machine Learning (ML) traditionally relied on centralized ``big data'' training, where data was collected and processed in the cloud. Although straightforward, this approach incurs high communication costs and privacy risks. To overcome these issues, distributed ``small data'' processing at the edge has gained traction, enabling real-time ML training with minimal data transfer. FL facilitates this by allowing agents to collaboratively train models while keeping data local, preserving privacy, and maintaining accuracy \cite{r20, r200}. Multi-agent reinforcement learning builds on FL by enabling distributed decision-making through deep networks \cite{r21}, but its application in large-scale wireless systems is hampered by significant communication overhead. Personalized Federated Learning (PFL) addresses non-IID data issues by enabling federated multitask learning \cite{r22, r89}, allowing devices to perform correlated but distinct tasks. Despite these advances, there remains a need for adaptive and efficient distributed learning methods that maximize data utility while ensuring privacy.

% Traditionally, Machine Learning (ML) models were trained using a centralized ``big data" approach, where large volumes of data were collected and processed in a central cloud. While simple to implement, this approach incurs significant communication overhead and raises severe privacy concerns, as data must be transmitted to the centralized model. These limitations have led to a shift towards distributed ``small data" processing at the edge, allowing devices to rapidly access and process real-time data for faster ML training. FL exemplifies this approach by enabling agents to collaboratively execute a learning task by sharing only their local model parameters, thereby preserving data privacy and achieving high accuracy \cite{r20, r200}. Following initial FL research, numerous techniques have been developed to address specific challenges. Currently, multi-agent reinforcement learning has gained popularity by combining reinforcement learning with deep neural networks and distributed control, allowing agents to solve optimization problems without relying on global information \cite{r21}. However, both FL and MARL must operate over large-scale wireless systems, which have issues such as extensive overhead. PFL, using federated multitask learning \cite{r22, r89}, addresses the challenge of non-IID data by enabling devices to implement correlated but distinct learning tasks. There remains a need for adaptive, robust, and efficient distributed learning approaches that leverage all available data without compromising privacy.

To further improve the efficiency of distributed edge environments, the authors of \cite{r9} investigated data-private CL methods in the medical science domain, adopting a sequential training approach across institutions rather than parallel training. In this method, each institution trains the model before passing it to the next, continuing until all have completed the process. However, this serial approach can lead to catastrophic forgetting, where the model favors the most recently seen data \cite{r10}. An effective alternative involves iterative computation of model weight updates from batches that combine data across multiple non-IID institutional datasets, rather than averaging updates from institution-specific batches \cite{r23}. A recent paper proposes a collaborative distributed Network Data Analytics
Function (NWDAF) architecture for B5G, enhancing NF data localization, security, and training efficiency while reducing control overhead and improving accuracy through real-time network data testing \cite{r24}. However, this architecture includes additional global model training logical functions for each NF, which increases network overhead and cost. In particular, to the best of our knowledge, no prior work has addressed network traffic prediction within the CL architecture.



\subsection{Traffic forecasting}
% ML techniques have been actively explored as a significant research area in recent years, particularly for predicting network traffic. This subsection covers a range of key studies related to traffic prediction. The authors of \cite{r25} have proposed an ensemble strategy utilizing bagging and a Light Gradient-Boosting Machine for this purpose. Furthermore, a review highlights that the accuracy of ML-based traffic prediction models relies heavily on the dataset used, making them sensitive to changes in traffic patterns \cite{r26}. Another related work explored the application of linear ML models and ensembles for predicting mobile traffic in real-world wireless networks, underscoring the need for data preprocessing despite the advantages of interpretability and fast training speeds of ML models \cite{r27}. While these studies have demonstrated the potential of ML models to improve the accuracy of network traffic predictions, they also show limitations when handling large datasets and recognizing complex patterns. Therefore, advanced techniques are needed to boost prediction accuracy and make better use of data.

% Addressing the limitations of traditional ML methods, researchers are shifting towards time-series DL techniques for network traffic prediction. Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs), Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM), and Gated Recurrent Units (GRU) are widely employed due to their proficiency in capturing temporal patterns in data. One study, \cite{r28}, examines the use of LSTM for forecasting network traffic and demonstrates its better performance in capturing complex patterns and dependencies in traffic data. Another deep meta-learning framework using LSTM has been proposed for the same prediction task, ensuring high accuracy while optimizing the number of samples and epochs required for model training \cite{r29}. Additionally, an enhanced GRU network has been proposed for predicting satellite network traffic, utilizing time-series deep learning techniques to address the shortcomings of traditional ML methods and accurately forecast mobile traffic patterns \cite{r30}. However, because network traffic data encompasses both temporal and spatial patterns, these time-series methods struggle to capture the complexity of network traffic data.

% Convolutional DL techniques have been applied to mobile traffic prediction tasks to exploit spatiotemporal patterns robustly. In \cite{r31}, graph convolution and LSTM models have been employed to capture the characteristics of cellular networks, competently capturing spatiotemporal features and significantly enhancing prediction accuracy. Similarly, network traffic is predicted across an environment with 470 access points, and the results indicate that CNN-LSTM and CNN-GRU models reliably capture spatiotemporal features, aligning with the findings of other related studies \cite{r32}. CNN-LSTM is also utilized in \cite{r33} to capture the spatiotemporal characteristics of network traffic for precise forecasting. These studies demonstrate that convolutional DL techniques have the potential to capture complex patterns, enhancing the accuracy of mobile traffic prediction. However, training methods that take into account the patterns and characteristics of the data are necessary for efficient utilization of vast real-world data and performance improvement. To sum up, accurate traffic prediction is the key for operators to reduce their OpEx by intelligently allocating resources, including DU servers.

ML techniques have gained significant attention for network traffic prediction, leveraging models such as ensemble methods and tree-based algorithms. For instance, an ensemble strategy incorporating bagging and a light gradient boosting machine has been proposed to enhance prediction accuracy \cite{r25}. Similarly, the sensitivity of ML-based traffic prediction models to dataset variations has been highlighted, emphasizing the impact of data quality on model performance \cite{r26}. Furthermore, linear ML models and ensembles have been explored for mobile traffic prediction, demonstrating the need for data preprocessing despite their interpretability and efficiency \cite{r27}. However, traditional ML models struggle with complex traffic patterns and datasets, which require more advanced techniques.

% To overcome these limitations, researchers have turned to deep learning, particularly time-series models like RNNs, LSTMs, and GRUs, which effectively capture temporal dependencies in network traffic data. LSTM-based approaches have shown superior performance in learning complex dependencies while optimizing training efficiency \cite{r28, r29}. Similarly, an enhanced GRU model has been introduced for satellite network traffic prediction, further demonstrating the effectiveness of deep learning in this domain \cite{r30}. However, since traffic data also exhibits spatial correlations, time-series models alone fail to fully capture its complexity. Convolutional deep learning techniques address this limitation by leveraging spatiotemporal patterns, as demonstrated in \cite{r31}, where graph convolution with LSTM significantly improves prediction accuracy by capturing cellular network characteristics.

