\begin{abstract}
For any smooth sphere eversion, the number of quadruple point jumps is always odd.  
%Recently, a realization with exactly one such jump has been obtained ({\color{red}{To be confirmed}}).  
In this paper, we introduce a function that detects the first quadruple point jump as the eversion starts. 
%Our function is of sphere immersions by raising both the domain and codomain dimensions by one and is invariant under local moves by Goryunov.   
The invariant is a higher-dimensional analogue of Arnold strangeness invariant for plane curves.   
\end{abstract}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}\label{Intro}
Smale \cite{Smale1959} showed that the unit sphere in $\mathbb{R}^3$ is regular homotopic to its reflection.  Later, a realization of such a homotopy became well known.  
In particular, for any smooth sphere eversion, the number of quadruple point jumps is always odd \cite{NelsonBanchoff1981}.  Given this, it is quite natural to consider mathematical tools, i.e. topological invariants, to detect at which quadruple point the eversion begins a jump.   In this paper, we introduce an invariant $\St$ for sphere immersions $S^2 \looparrowright \mathbb{R}^3$, by extending the Arnold strangeness invariant  \cite{Arnold1994Book} of plane curves to higher dimensions, raising both the domain and the target dimensions by one.  
 We begin with our main result.  
\begin{theorem}\label{thmSphere}
Let $S$ be a sphere immersion $S^2 \looparrowright \mathbb{R}^3$ and let $T(S)$ be the set of triple points of $S$. 
Then   
\[\St(S)=\sum_{t \in T(S)} \ind(t)
 \mod 2
\]
changes by one under a single quadruple point jump as in Figure~\ref{fig:Qmove}, and remains unchanged under jumps of singularities:  \textup{(E)}, \textup{(H)}, and \textup{(T)} as in Figures~\ref{fig:Emove}--\ref{fig:Tmove} of Section~\ref{sec:Def}.  
\end{theorem}
\noindent Here,   the value $\ind(t)$ of a triple point~$t$ is defined as the average of the values of the  Alexander numbering---viewed as a map from the set of connected components of $\mathbb{R}^3 \setminus S(S^2)$ to~$\mathbb{Z}$---on the eight regions that are locally adjacent to~$t$ (see the latter part of Section~\ref{sec:Def}).    

The plan of this paper is as follows.  
In Section~\ref{sec:Def}, we prepare definitions to define our invariant $\St$, following Goryunov \cite{Goryunov1997}.   
In Section~\ref{sec:MainR}, we state our main result.  
In Section~\ref{sec:Proof}, we give a proof of the result in Section~\ref{sec:MainR}.  
%While the main application of our invariant is the detection of the unique or an odd number of  quadruple point jumps in the realization of a sphere eversion, we also mention other applications.  
While the main result concerns $\bmod~2$, almost all of the proof is written over the field $\mathbb{Q}$, since the use of a half-integer valued weight function provides a clearer view of the argument.  

We remark here that an extension of Theorem~\ref{thmSphere} to a $\mathbb{Q}$-valued invariant, analogous to the integer-valued invariant of Shumakovich \cite{Shumakovich1995}, will be discussed in a separate paper currently being prepared by the authors.   

\section{Definitions}\label{sec:Def}
\begin{definition}
A smooth immersion from a closed oriented surface into $\mathbb{R}^3$ is said to be \emph{generic} if its image locally exhibits only transverse intersections of two or three smooth sheets.
%A \emph{generic immersion} is a smooth map from a closed oriented surface into $\mathbb{R}^3$  whose derivative is injective at every point, and whose image locally exhibits only transverse intersections of two or three smooth sheets.
%Two generic immersions are said to be \emph{regular homotopic} if there exists a homotopy between them in which the differential has rank 2 at all times.
%In this context, simple singularities such as self-tangencies and quadruple points are allowed to occur during the deformation.
%It is known that any two regular homotopic generic immersions can be transformed into each other by a sequence of four {\color{black}{local}} moves, each corresponding to a singularity jump:
%{\color{red}{EHTQのmoveの$4$枚の画像を挿入}}
\end{definition}
%In Goryunov ~\cite{Goryunov1997}, seven types of local moves are considered: the four jumps \textup{(E)}, \textup{(H)}, \textup{(T)}, and \textup{(Q)}, together with three additional moves associated with pinch points. Among them, the four types \textup{(E)}, \textup{(H)}, \textup{(T)}, and \textup{(Q)} correspond to codimension-one jumps in the  space of generic immersions.  Hence, in this paper, keeping Smale's sphere eversion in mind, we focus exclusively on these four types and do not consider the additional moves involving pinch points.  

As long as there is no risk of confusion, we simply identify a generic immersion with its image. 

Let $\mathcal{F}:=C^\infty (\Sigma, \mathbb{R}^3)$ denote the space of smooth maps from a closed oriented surface $\Sigma$ into $\mathbb{R}^3$, and let $\Omega \subset \mathcal{F}$ denote the open dense subspace of generic immersions.  The complement  $\Delta=\mathcal{F} \setminus \Omega$ is called the \emph{discriminant}.  A path $\gamma : [0, 1] \to \mathcal{F}$ from $\gamma(0)$ to $\gamma(1)$ is said to be \emph{generic} if $\gamma$ intersects $\Delta$ transversely at finitely many points.   According to Goryunov~\cite{Goryunov1997}, each such intersection point corresponds to a singular map at which one of the four codimension-one jumps, namely \textup{(E)}, \textup{(H)}, \textup{(T)}, or  \textup{(Q)} occurs: 

