\begin{definition}
Two generic immersions $f$ and $g$ in $\Omega$ are said to be \emph{generically  regularly homotopic} if there is a generic path in $\mathcal{F}$ connecting them.  Equivalently, $f$ and $g$ are connected by a \emph{generic regular homotopy} if they can be transformed by a finite sequence of diffeomorphisms and the four local jumps \textup{(E)}, \textup{(H)}, \textup{(T)}, and \textup{(Q)}.   
\end{definition}
Hence, we study generic immersions under generic regular homotopy.  The formulation we adopt here reflects our focus on Smale's sphere eversion.  In contrast to the paper ~\cite{Goryunov1997}, which includes additional local moves involving pinch points, we restrict our attention to those singularities encountered in generic one-parameter families of immersions.  That is, our category of interest is generated entirely by generic immersions and generic regular homotopies arising from the four codimension-one jumps and diffeomorphisms.   
%Thus, any generic path in $\mathcal{F}$ between generic immersions can be decomposed into a finite sequence of these four types of local jumps of singularities.    We remark that a general regular homotopy may pass through more complicated singularities including $n$-fold points for $n \ge 5$, which lie in higher-codimension strata of the discriminant.  Such singularities do not occur in a generic one-parameter family of immersions.  Therefore, they are not among the four elementary jumps \textup{(E)}, \textup{(H)}, \textup{(T)}, and  \textup{(Q)}, which correspond to codimension-one strata of the discriminant.  
%===========

Given a generic immersion, $\mathbb{R}^3$ is decomposed into the image of the immersion and its complement.  Moreover, the image of a generic immersion consists of  sheets, \emph{double lines} (intersections of two sheets), and \emph{triple points} (intersections of three sheets).  
%A  double line between two triple points is called an \emph{edge}.  Also, a portion of a sheet bounded by some triple points and several edges is called a \emph{face}.  
A connected  component of the complement of the image of a generic immersion is called a  \emph{region}.    
The coorientation of a generic immersion 
 is induced by the orientation of the domain surface. 
For a generic immersion, we assign numbers to the set of regions  based on its coorientation (Figure~\ref{fig:coori}).   
\begin{figure}[htbp] %  figure placement: here, top, bottom, or page
\includegraphics[width=5cm]{coorientation.pdf}
    \vspace{-1cm}
   \caption{A coorientation of a sheet and indices.  The direction of the coorientation of the sheet is indicated by short segments.}
   \label{fig:coori}
\end{figure}
More precisely, the assignment proceeds as follows.  First, we assign $-\frac{1}{2}$ to the region containing the point at $\infty$.  Then, we increase or decrease the number along the coorientation.  In this way, we assign numbers to the complement of the image of generic immersion, and this defines the map $\ind : \{ \textrm{regions} \} \to \mathbb{Z}-\frac{1}{2}$, called the \emph{Alexander numbering}.     
\begin{definition}\label{def:opposite}
%{(\color{red}{Definition of opposite has moved here.})}
Any triple point is formed by three sheets (see Figure~\ref{fig:8-regions}).  Then two regions adjacent to a triple point $t$ are said to be \emph{opposite} if they are separated by exactly three sheets. 

For example, when we consider the triple point at the origin in $\mathbb{R}^3$ formed by the three coordinate planes $x=0$, $y=0$, and $z=0$, the regions containing  $(-1,-1,-1)$ and $(1,1,1)$ are opposite to each other.
\end{definition}
\begin{definition}\label{def:ind}
Let $\Sigma$ be a closed oriented surface,  
let $S$ be a generic immersion $S \colon \Sigma \looparrowright \mathbb{R}^3$, and let $T(S)$ be the set of triple points of $S$.   
We define a function $T(S) \to  \mathbb{Z};$ $t \mapsto \ind(t)$  
%$\ind(t) \in \frac{1}{2} \mathbb{Z}$ 
%for the triple point $t$ that it is defined as 
by averaging the values of $\ind$ over the eight regions that are locally adjacent to~$t$ (see Figure~\ref{fig:8-regions}, Lemma~\ref{lem:ind}).   The value $\ind(t)$ is called the \emph{index} of a triple point $t$.   
%In the same way, the index of an edge $e$ is the average of the values $\ind(r)$ assigned to the regions adjacent to $e$.  
\end{definition}
%\begin{example}
%The three coordinate planes $x=0$, $y=0$, and $z=0$ divide $\mathbb{R}^3$ into eight regions, each corresponding to a choice of signs $(\pm, \pm, \pm)$ for the coordinates. 
%\end{example}
We note that this definition is equivalent to defining $\ind(t)$ as the average of the values of $\ind$ over a pair of opposite regions adjacent to the triple point t; although there are four such choices, they all give the same result.  The fact will be useful when computing the change in $\St$ under the move \textup{(Q)}.

We note also that while the function $\St \bmod 2$, stated in our main result (Theorem~\ref{thmSurface}), is defined as a sum over triple points, the contribution from each triple point is a rational number. 
Hence, it is natural that the discussion in Section~\ref{sec:Proof} takes place over $\mathbb{Q}$.  The function $\St$, expressed as a sum of indices, yields the main result reduction modulo $2$.  

\section{Main result}\label{sec:MainR}
We state the main result (Theorem~\ref{thmSurface}), which generalizes Theorem~\ref{thmSphere} by extending the domain $S^2$ to an arbitrary closed oriented surface $\Sigma$.  
\begin{theorem}\label{thmSurface}
Let $\Sigma$ be a closed oriented surface and let $S$ be a generic  immersion $S : \Sigma \looparrowright \mathbb{R}^3$.    Let $T(S)$ be the set of triple points of $S$ and let $\ind$ be as in Definition~\ref{def:ind}.  
We define a $\mathbb{Q}$-valued function  
\[\St (S)=\sum_{t \in T(S)} \ind(t).    
\]
Then every value
$\St(S)$ is an integer and moreover $\St(S) \bmod 2$ changes by one under a single quadruple point jump \textup{(Q)}  as in Figure~\ref{fig:Qmove}, and remains unchanged under jumps of singularities:  \textup{(E)}, \textup{(H)}, and \textup{(T)} as in Figures~\ref{fig:Emove}--\ref{fig:Tmove} of Section~\ref{sec:Def}, where $\ind(t)$ is an index associated to  $t$, defined in Section~\ref{sec:Def}.     
\end{theorem}

\section{Proofs}\label{sec:Proof}
In order to prove Theorem~\ref{thmSurface},  
we consider  Propositions~\ref{Prop:Tmove} and \ref{Prop:Qmove}, and Lemmas~\ref{lem:ind} and \ref{lem:oprg}, which  concern the jumps \textup{(T)} and \textup{(Q)}---singularity jumps involving triple points.   
\begin{prop}\label{Prop:Tmove} 
      $\Stwo$ is preserved under the jump \textup{(T)}. 
  \end{prop}
  \begin{proof}
    To prove this proposition, it suffices to compare the difference between $\St(S_0)$ before and $\St(S_1)$ after the jump \textup{(T)}.  The two triple points $t$ and $t'$ that appear in the jump \textup{(T)} have the same value of $\ind$   
    %their contributions cancel each other out
    (see Figure~\ref{fig:tript_Tmove}).  
\begin{figure}[htbp] %  figure placement: here, top, bottom, or page
       \includegraphics[width=8cm]{tript_Tmove.pdf} 
       \caption{Two triple points $t$ and $t'$ that appear in a single jump \textup{(T)}.}
       \label{fig:tript_Tmove}
    \end{figure}
   % {\color{red}{Tmoveで出てくるtriplepointの図を挿入．}}
In other words, the following holds 
    \begin{align*}
     \St(S_1) - \St(S_0) &= \left(\ind(t) + \ind(t')+\St(S_0)\right)-\St(S_0) \\
     &= \ind(t) + \ind(t') \\ 
   &= 2 \ind(t)  \\
     &\equiv 0 \quad (\bmod 2).    
\end{align*}
The last congruence modulo $2$ holds by Lemma~\ref{lem:ind}. 
  \end{proof} 
  \begin{lemma}\label{lem:ind}
    Let $t$ be a triple point and let $d$ be the minimum of the indices of the eight regions adjacent to $t$.  Then \[\ind(t) = d + \frac{3}{2}, \]
and in particular,  
\[
\ind(t) \in \mathbb{Z}.  
\]
  \end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
By using the coorientation of the three sheets that form the triple point $t$, the indices of the eight regions adjacent to $t$ are automatically determined.  Therefore, the indices of regions adjacent to $t$ are $d, d+1, d+1, d+1, d+2, d+2, d+2,$ and $d+3$ (see Figure~\ref{fig:8-regions}).  
\begin{figure}[htbp] 
   \includegraphics[width=8cm]{8Regions.pdf} 
   \caption{The indices of the regions adjacent to $t$ are $d, d+1, d+1, d+1, d+2, d+2, d+2,$ and $d+3$.}
   \label{fig:8-regions}
\end{figure}
    Thus, 
    \[
    \ind(t) = \frac{1}{8} \left(d + d+1 + d+1 + d+1 + d+2 + d+2 + d+2 + d+3 \right) = d + \frac{3}{2}.
    \]
To show this is always an integer, recall  that we assign $-\frac{1}{2}$ to the region containing the point at $\infty$.  
Since $d \in \mathbb{Z}-\frac{1}{2}$, the value $\ind(t)=d+\frac{3}{2}$ lies in $\mathbb{Z}$. 
  \end{proof}
Before discussing the change of the values of the $\St$ under any  jump \textup{(Q)}, let us mention Lemma~\ref{lem:oprg} that will help in calculating the difference in $\St$. 
It is technically convenient when  analyzing the changes under the jump \textup{(Q)}, to express the value $\ind(t)$ in terms of the indices of the opposite regions $r$ and $\hat{r}$  rather than using the minimal value.  
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:oprg}
The difference in $\ind$ values between two opposite regions adjacent to a triple point is determined by the coorientation of the three sheets that form the triple point.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Suppose that the indices of the regions adjacent to  $t$ are $d, d+1, d+1, d+1, d+2, d+2, d+2,$ and $d+3$.  Then the path from $r$ to $\hat{r}$ is classified into four cases,  according to the pair $\ind(r) \to \ind(\hat{r})$ as: 
\begin{itemize}
\item $d \to d+3$ (one path), 
\item $d+1 \to d+2$ (three paths), 
\item $d+2 \to d+1$ (three paths), 
\item $d+3 \to d$ (one path).  
\end{itemize}
There are exactly eight such paths, since there are eight regions adjacent to a given triple point $t$.  
This observation implies the claim.  
\end{proof}
\begin{notation}\label{Not:Qmove}
By definition, any jump \textup{(Q)} involves four sheets, among which three form a triple point, and the fourth sheet passes through the triple point.  By  Lemma~\ref{lem:oprg}, we introduce a refined notation to specify the four types of jump \textup{(Q)},  based on the direction in which the moving sheet $A$ passes from a region $r$ to its opposite region $\hat{r}$.  More concretely, 
\begin{itemize}
\item when $d \to d+3$, we call it a $\textup{Q}_3$-move, 
\item when $d+1 \to d+2$, we call it a $\textup{Q}_2$-move,
\item when $d+2 \to d+1$, we call it a $\textup{Q}_1$-move,
\item when $d+3 \to d$, we call it a $\textup{Q}_0$-move.  
\end{itemize}
\end{notation}
\begin{prop}\label{Prop:Qmove}
Let $S_0$ and $S_1$ be two generic immersions such that $S_1$ is obtained from $S_0$ by a \textup{Q}$_i$-move, where \textup{Q}$_i$-move is defined in ~Notation~\ref{Not:Qmove} $(i=0, 1, 2, 3)$.  
  The function $\St$ changes  under the jumps \textup{(Q)}. More precisely, 
  \[
\St (S_1) -\St(S_0) =
\begin{cases}
3 & \text{under \textup{Q}$_{3}$-move}  \\
1 & \text{under \textup{Q}$_{2}$-move}  \\
-1 & \text{under \textup{Q}$_{1}$-move}  \\
-3 & \text{under \textup{Q}$_{0}$-move} .
\end{cases}
\]
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
Let $X$, $Y$, and $Z$ denote the three sheets that form the triple point appearing in the Q$_i$-move ($i=0, 1, 2, 3$), and let $A$ and $A'$ denote the moving sheets.  Let $t_{X,Y,Z}$ be the triple point formed by $\{X, Y, Z\}$.   Similarly, define $t_{A,X,Y}$, $t_{A,Y,Z}$, and $t_{A,Z,X}$ as the triple points formed by $\{A,X,Y\}$, $\{A,Y,Z\}$, and $\{A,Z,X\}$, respectively.
Fixing the coorientation of $A$ does not affect the generality of the argument.
Let $d$ denote the $\ind$ value of the tetrahedron appearing in the figure before the Q$_i$-move.
  The pattern of change in $\St$ depends on the coorientations of the sheets $X$, $Y$, and $Z$.  Hence, 
  we consider four possible cases, described in Notation~\ref{Not:Qmove}.  

Let us first consider the Q$_3$-move. By the definition of coorientations of sheets, the distribution of $\ind$ values over the regions before and after the Q$_3$-move is illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:DiffQ3}.  
\begin{figure}[htbp] %  figure placement: here, top, bottom, or page
    \vspace{-3cm}
 \includegraphics[width=16cm]{Q3-move.pdf}
 \vspace{-1cm}
 \caption{The indices for a single Q$_3$-move.}
   \label{fig:DiffQ3}
\end{figure}
In particular, the indices take the following values:
\[
  \begin{alignedat}{6}
    \ind(t_{A',X,Y}) &= d + \frac{3}{2} ,&\quad
      \ind(t_{A',Y,Z}) &= d + \frac{3}{2} ,&\quad
      \ind(t_{A',Z,X}) &= d + \frac{3}{2} ,\\
    \ind(t_{A,X,Y})  &= d + \frac{1}{2} ,&\quad
      \ind(t_{A,Y,Z})  &= d + \frac{1}{2} ,&\quad
      \ind(t_{A,Z,X})  &= d + \frac{1}{2}.
  \end{alignedat}
\]
  The variation of $\St$ across the Q$_3$-move is as follows:
\begin{align*}
  &\St(S_1) - \St(S_0)\\
  &\begin{array}{ccccccc}
=& \ind(t_{A',X,Y}) &+ \ind(t_{A',Y,Z}) &+ \ind(t_{A',Z,X}) 
&- \ind(t_{A,X,Y}) &- \ind(t_{A,Y,Z}) &- \ind(t_{A,Z,X}) \\
\rule{0pt}{4ex}
=& d + \dfrac{3}{2} &+ d + \dfrac{3}{2} &+ d + \dfrac{3}{2}
&- d - \dfrac{1}{2} &- d - \dfrac{1}{2} &- d - \dfrac{1}{2}
\end{array}\\
&\quad =3.
\end{align*}

Next, we consider the second case, namely the Q$_2$-move. 
This case is obtained from the Q$_3$-move by reversing the coorientation of one of the sheets $\{X,Y,Z \}$. Due to the symmetry around the triple point $t_{X,Y,Z}$, the choice of which sheet to reverse does not affect the generality of the argument.  Let us assume that the coorientation of the sheet $X$ is reversed.  In this case, the values of $\ind$ assigned to the regions differ from those in the case of the Q$_3$-move. Using  Lemma~\ref{lem:ind}, we determine the distribution of $\ind$ values over the regions, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:DiffQ2}.   
\begin{figure}
\vspace{-3cm}
\includegraphics[width=16cm]{Q2-move.pdf}
\vspace{-1cm}
\caption{Indices for a single Q$_2$-move.}\label{fig:DiffQ2}
\end{figure}
The values of $\ind$ of triple points in this distribution are as follows:
 \[
  \begin{alignedat}{6}
    \ind(t_{A',X,Y}) &= d + \frac{1}{2} ,&\quad
      \ind(t_{A',Y,Z}) &= d - \frac{1}{2} ,&\quad
      \ind(t_{A',Z,X}) &= d + \frac{1}{2} ,\\
    \ind(t_{A,X,Y})  &= d - \frac{1}{2} ,&\quad
      \ind(t_{A,Y,Z})  &= d + \frac{1}{2} ,&\quad
      \ind(t_{A,Z,X})  &= d - \frac{1}{2}.
  \end{alignedat}
\]
  Therefore, we have the following:
  \begin{align*}
    &\St(S_1) - \St(S_0)\\
     &\begin{array}{ccccccc}
       =& \ind(t_{A',X,Y}) &+ \ind(t_{A',Y,Z}) &+ \ind(t_{A',Z,X}) 
       &- \ind(t_{A,X,Y}) &- \ind(t_{A,Y,Z}) &- \ind(t_{A,Z,X}) \\
       \rule{0pt}{4ex}
       =& d + \dfrac{1}{2} &+ d - \dfrac{1}{2} &+ d + \dfrac{1}{2}
       &-  d + \dfrac{1}{2}  &- d - \dfrac{1}{2} &-  d + \dfrac{1}{2}.
     \end{array}\\
     &\quad=1.
 \end{align*}

Similarly, we compute the remaining two cases.  In the case of the Q$_1$-move, the coorientations of two sheets are reversed from the Q$_3$ case (or equivalently, one sheet is reversed from the Q$_2$ case).  The corresponding  distribution of the values of $\ind$ is illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:DiffQ1}. 
\begin{figure}
    \vspace{-3cm}
    \includegraphics[width=16cm]{Q1-move.pdf}
    \vspace{-1cm}
    \caption{Indices for a single Q$_1$-move.}\label{fig:DiffQ1}
\end{figure}
This figure shows the case where the coorientations of sheets $X$ and $Y$ are reversed.  Based on the distribution, the values of $\ind$ of triple points are computed as follows: 
  \[
  \begin{alignedat}{6}
    \ind(t_{A',X,Y}) &= d - \frac{1}{2} ,&\quad
      \ind(t_{A',Y,Z}) &= d - \frac{3}{2} ,&\quad
      \ind(t_{A',Z,X}) &= d - \frac{3}{2} ,\\
    \ind(t_{A,X,Y})  &= d - \frac{3}{2} ,&\quad
      \ind(t_{A,Y,Z})  &= d - \frac{1}{2} ,&\quad
      \ind(t_{A,Z,X})  &= d - \frac{1}{2}.
  \end{alignedat}
\]

\begin{align*}
  &\St(S_1) - \St(S_0)\\
  &\begin{array}{ccccccc}
=& \ind(t_{A',X,Y}) &+ 
  \ind(t_{A',Y,Z}) &+ 
  \ind(t_{A',Z,X}) 
&- \ind(t_{A,X,Y}) &- 
  \ind(t_{A,Y,Z}) &- 
  \ind(t_{A,Z,X}) \\
\rule{0pt}{4ex}
=& d - \dfrac{1}{2} &+
 d - \dfrac{3}{2} &+
 d - \dfrac{3}{2} &-
 d + \dfrac{3}{2} &-
 d + \dfrac{1}{2} &-
 d + \dfrac{1}{2}.
\end{array}\\
&\quad=-1.
\end{align*}

  The case of the Q$_0$-move is obtained from the Q$_3$-move by reversing coorientations of $X$,$Y$, and $Z$.  
The distribution of the values of $\ind$ over regions in this case is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:DiffQ0}.  
\begin{figure}
    \vspace{-3cm}
    \includegraphics[width=16cm]{Q0-move.pdf}
    \vspace{-1cm}
    \caption{The indices for a single Q$_0$-move.}\label{fig:DiffQ0}
\end{figure}
As in the previous cases, we compute the $\ind$ of the triple points as follows:
 \[
  \begin{alignedat}{6}
    \ind(t_{A',X,Y}) &= d - \frac{5}{2} ,&\quad
      \ind(t_{A',Y,Z}) &= d - \frac{5}{2} ,&\quad
      \ind(t_{A',Z,X}) &= d - \frac{5}{2} ,\\
    \ind(t_{A,X,Y})  &= d - \frac{3}{2} ,&\quad
      \ind(t_{A,Y,Z})  &= d - \frac{3}{2} ,&\quad
      \ind(t_{A,Z,X})  &= d - \frac{3}{2}.
  \end{alignedat}
\]
  Consequently, we obtain: 
  \begin{align*}
    &\St(S_1) - \St(S_0)\\
    &\begin{array}{ccccccc}
  =& \ind(t_{A',X,Y}) &+ 
    \ind(t_{A',Y,Z}) &+ 
    \ind(t_{A',Z,X}) 
  &- \ind(t_{A,X,Y}) &- 
    \ind(t_{A,Y,Z}) &- 
    \ind(t_{A,Z,X}) \\
  \rule{0pt}{4ex}
  =& d - \dfrac{5}{2} &+
   d - \dfrac{5}{2} &+
   d - \dfrac{5}{2} &-
   d + \dfrac{3}{2} &-
   d + \dfrac{3}{2} &-
   d + \dfrac{3}{2}.
  \end{array}\\
  &\quad=-3.
  \end{align*}
 \end{proof}

We now complete a proof of Theorem~\ref{thmSurface}.  
\begin{proof}
First, $\St$ is preserved under any jump \textup{(E)} and any jump \textup{(H)}, since no triple points are involved in these moves.  
Second, by Proposition~\ref{Prop:Tmove}, $\Stwo$ is preserved under any jump \textup{(T)}.  
Third, Proposition~\ref{Prop:Qmove} implies that each jump \textup{(Q)} changes the value of $\St$ by an odd number, that is, the change is congruent to $1$ modulo $2$, which completes the proof of the claim.     
\end{proof}
