\title{ Kac-Rice inspired approach to non-Hermitian random matrices}

\author{\Large Yan V Fyodorov	\footnote{yan.fyodorov@kcl.ac.uk}\\[0.5ex] {\small Department of Mathematics, King's College London}\\
{\small Strand, London, WC2R 2LS, UK}\\ and \\{\small Faculty of Physics, Bielefeld University, Postfach 100131, 33501 Bielefeld, Germany}}


\date{\today}

\maketitle

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{abstract}
	We suggest a method of analyzing the joint probability density (JPD) ${\cal P}_N(z,{\bf v})$  of an eigenvalue $z$ and the associated right eigenvector ${\bf v}$ (normalized with ${\bf v}^*{\bf v}=1$) of non-Hermitian random matrices of a given size $N\times N$. The method, which represents an alternative to Girko Hermitization approach,  is essentially based on the Kac-Rice counting formula applied to the associated characteristic polynomial
combined with a certain integral identity for the Dirac delta function of such a polynomial. To illustrate utility of the general method we apply it to consider two particular cases:
(i)  one-parameter family of matrices interpolating between complex Ginibre and real Ginibre ensembles and (ii) a complex Ginibre matrix additively perturbed by a fixed matrix. In particular, in the former case we discuss the formation of an excess of eigenvalues in the vicinity of the real axis on approaching the real Ginibre limit,
which eventually gives rise to the existence of a new scaling regime of "weak non-reality" as $N\to \infty$. In the second case we provide new insights into 
eigenvalue and eigenvector distribution for a general rank one perturbation of complex Ginibre matrices of finite size $N$, and in the structure of an outlier as $N\gg 1$.  
Finally we discuss a possible generalization of the proposed method which is expected to be suitable for analysis of JPD involving both left- and right eigenvectors.





\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Let $\mathbf{x}$ be a $N-$ component column vector, real or complex.  We will use $\mathbf{x}^T=(x_1,\ldots, x_N)$ to denote the corresponding transposed row vector ( and similar notation for matrices), and $\mathbf{x}^*=(\overline{x}_1,\ldots, \overline{x}_N)$ for the Hermitian conjugate, with bar standing for complex conjugation. The inner product of two such vectors will be denoted  as $\mathbf{x}_1^*\mathbf{x}_2=\sum_{i=1}^N \overline{x}_{1i}x_{2i}$.

Let  $X$ be a $N\times N$ matrix which will be in general assumed to be non-selfadjoint and not normal : $X^*\ne X, \, X^{*}X\ne XX^{*}$. We will further assume that all $N$ eigenvalues $z_a, \, a=1,\ldots,N$ of this matrix, which are in general complex numbers, are distinct.% have multiplicity one.

 Then the matrix is diagonalizable by a similarity transformation: $X=S Z S^{-1}$ where $Z=\mbox{diag}\left(z_1,\ldots,z_N\right)$ and $S$ is in general non-unitary: $S^*\ne S^{-1}$. The associated {\it right} eigenvectors defined by  $X\,\mathbf{v}_{a}=z_a \mathbf{v}_{a}$ are columns of the matrix $S$, whereas their {\it left} counterparts
 satisfying  $\mathbf{u}_{a}^* X=z_a \mathbf{u}^*_{a}$ form the rows of $S^{-1}$, and generically $\mathbf{v}_{a}$ and $\mathbf{u}_{a}$ are not related, unless the matrix is complex symmetric:
 $X^T=X$. Non-unitarity of $S$ implies that $\mathbf{v}^*_{b}\mathbf{v}_{a}\ne \delta_{ab}$ and similarly $\mathbf{u}^*_{a}\mathbf{u}_{b}\ne \delta_{ab}$.
  The simplest informative object characterizing the so-called eigenvector non-orthogonality is the so-called 'overlap matrix' $\mathcal{O}_{ab}=\frac{(\mathbf{v}^*_{a}\mathbf{v}_{b})(\mathbf{u}^*_{b}\mathbf{u}_{a})}{|\mathbf{v}^*_{a}\mathbf{u}_{b}|^2}$.  In particular, the real diagonal entries $\mathcal{O}_{aa}$ are
  known in the literature on numerical analysis as {\it eigenvalue condition numbers} and characterize sensitivity of eigenvalues $\lambda_a$ to an additive perturbation of entries of $X$, see e.g. \cite{TrefethenBook}, with $\mathcal{O}_{aa}$ controlling the speed of change of eigenvalues under the perturbation.
The Cauchy-Schwarz inequality
implies  $\mathcal{O}_{aa}\ge 1$, and one may show that actually for some classes of non-normal matrices of large size $N\gg 1$ one typically has $\mathcal{O}_{aa}\gg 1$, so that their eigenvalues could be much more sensitive to perturbations in comparison with their normal counterparts.
In fact the two sets $\mathbf{v}^*_{a}$ and $\mathbf{u}_{a}$ of right and left eigenvectors  can always be chosen to satisfy the {\it bi-orthonormality} condition $\mathbf{v}^*_{a}\mathbf{u}_{b}=\delta_{ab}$ for $a,b=1,\ldots,N$, and such a condition is frequently assumed in the literature.

  If the matrix $X$ is random, it makes sense to be interested in its statistical characteristics. In what follows we will use the notation $\left \langle \ldots \right\rangle_{X}$ to denote the expectation with respect to any specified ensemble of matrices $X$.
   In this way, for example the brackets $\left \langle \ldots \right\rangle_{GinUE}$ will represent the expectation taken with respect to the probability measure on $G$ known as the complex Ginibre ensemble, denoted here as $GinUE$ to reflect its invariance with respect to complex unitary conjugation. Its counterpart with real entries known as the real Ginibre ensemble will be denoted correspondingly with $GinOE$, to reflect invariance with respect to conjugation by real orthogonal matrices.

In our treatment we will liberally use the Dirac $\delta-$function $\delta(x), \, x\in \mathbb{R}$ notation, tacitly assuming an appropriate regularization for this generalized function, most frequently via the limit of a Gaussian:
\begin{equation}\label{Gaudelta}
\delta(x)=\lim_{\epsilon\to 0}\delta_{g,\epsilon}(x), \quad  \delta_{g,\epsilon}(x)=
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi \epsilon}}e^{-\frac{x^2}{2\epsilon}}=\int_{\mathbb{R}}e^{-\epsilon\frac{k^2}{2}+ikx}\, \frac{dk}{2\pi}
\end{equation}
unless otherwise specified. Such definitions as well as subsequent {\it distributional identities} involving the Dirac $\delta-$functions will be understood to hold in the sense of first integrating the regularized versions against suitably chosen test functions and then letting regularization to zero, see an example of such a procedure in
the Appendix A, Section (\ref{Zirn}). Further, for $z=x+iy\in \mathbb{C}$ we use the notation $\delta^{(2)}(z):=\delta(x)\delta(y)$ for the  Dirac delta-distribution in the complex plane, such that the empirical density of eigenvalues at a (in general, complex) point $z$ is given by $\rho_N(z)=\sum_{a=1}^N \delta^{(2)}(z-z_a)$. Such empirical
density is the main object characterizing position of eigenvalues of a given instance of a matrix.
Similarly one may consider the joint empirical density
of the eigenvalue and the corresponding right eigenvector ${\bf v}$. For this one needs to define each right eigenvector ${\bf v}_a$ uniquely, which can be done e.g. by imposing a normalization condition ${\bf v}_a^*{\bf v}_a=1$ and further fixing the phase $\theta_a$ of the first entry of each eigenvector: $v_{1a}=|v_{1a}|e^{i\theta_a}$  to the same value: $\theta_a=\theta \in [0,2\pi), \forall a$. Denoting such set of eigenvectors by ${\bf v}_a(\theta)$
  one then defines the joint empirical density with respect to the Lebesgue measure $d^2zd^{2N}{\bf v}$ via
\begin{equation}\label{empir_right_eigenv}
\Pi^{(L)}_{N,\theta}\left(z,{\bf v}\right)=\sum_{a=1}^N \delta^{(2)}(z-z_a)\delta^{(2N)}\left({\bf v}-{\bf v}_a(\theta)\right),
\end{equation}
with $\delta^{(2N)}({\bf v}):=\prod_{i=1}^N\delta^{(2)}(v_i)$ for any complex-valued vector ${\bf v}^T=(v_1,\ldots,v_N)$. In this paper we will find it
more convenient to consider the joint empirical density further averaged over the immaterial parameter $\theta$, i.e. 
$\Pi^{(L)}_N\left(z,{\bf v}\right):=\int_0^{2\pi}\Pi^{(L)}_{N,\theta}\left(z,{\bf v}\right)\frac{d\theta}{2\pi}$. 
The empirical density of eigenvalues is simply the corresponding marginal: $\rho_N(z)=\int_{\mathbb{C}^N} \Pi^{(L)}_N\left(z,{\bf v}\right)d^{2N}{\bf v}$.
Finally, since the eigenvectors are actually constrained to the unit complex sphere  $\mathbf{S}^c_N: {\bf v}^{*}{\bf v}=1, \, {\bf v}\in \mathbb{C}^N$ , it appears to be more natural to rather work with the joint empirical density with respect to the measure $d^2zd\mu_H({\bf v})$, where $d\mu_H({\bf v})$ (equivalent to the invariant Haar's measure) can be explicitly defined via $d\mu_H({\bf v})=C_N\delta({\bf v}^*{\bf v}-1)d^{2N}{\bf v}$, with the choice of $C_N$ ensuring normalization  $\int_{\mathbf{S}^c_N}d\mu_H({\bf v})=1$. The ensuing joint empirical density will be denoted simply as $\Pi_N\left(z,{\bf v}\right)$ in terms of which $\rho_N(z)=\int_{\mathbf{S}^c_N} \Pi_N\left(z,{\bf v}\right)d\mu_H({\bf v})$.

The moments and correlation functions of these empirical densities obtained by averaging over the specified ensemble of matrices $X$ are the main general objects of study in the theory of random matrices (RMT). To this end we will call the expectation $p_N(z)=\left\langle\rho_N(z)\right\rangle_X$ the mean eigenvalue density, while the expectation ${\cal P}_N\left(z,{\bf v}\right)= \frac{1}{N}\left\langle\Pi_N\left(z,{\bf v}\right)\right\rangle_X$ represents the joint probability density (JPD) of the eigenvalue and the corresponding right eigenvector, properly normalized as $\int_{\mathbb{C}}  \int_{\mathbf{S}^c_N} {\cal P}_N\left(z,{\bf v}\right)d\mu_H({\bf v})d^2z=1$, with further possibility of extension to the JPD of both right- and left eigenvectors.

The number of approaches allowing one to successfully address spectral properties of non-Hermitian non-normal matrices is relatively limited.
They essentially can be divided into two groups. The first approach relies upon either the Schur decomposition $X=U(Z+T)U^{*}$, where $U$ is unitary, $Z$ diagonal and $T$ is (upper) triangular, or its {\it incomplete} counterpart introduced by Edelman and collaborators in
\cite{EKS} and \cite{Ed:97} and adapted for addressing eigenvector properties in \cite{Fyo2018}. Although originally applied in the framework of classical Ginibre ensembles, recently that approach proved to be also very efficient in demonstrating the universality of obtained results for matrices with independent, identically distributed entries, see   \cite{Maltsev_Osman_2024, Osman2024_1,Osman2024_2,Osman2025}.

A different approach employs a trick going back to \cite{Girko1984} and
known as the {\it Girko Hermitization}: given a non-Hermitian $X$ of size $N$ one considers the Hermitian $2N\times 2N$ matrix
\begin{equation}\label{Hermitized}
Y_X(z,\overline{z})=\left(\begin{array}{cc} 0 &  \left(z{\bf 1}_N-X\right)\\  \left(z{\bf 1}_N-X\right)^{*} & 0\end{array}\right).
\end{equation}
Its utility comes from the fact that $z$ is an eigenvalue of $X$ if and only if the matrix  $Y_X(z,\overline{z})$ has a nontrivial kernel.
The empirical density of complex eigenvalues for $X$ can be recovered from the fundamental distributional identity
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{4\pi}\partial_{\overline{z}}\partial_{z}\log{\det{Y_X(z,\overline{z})}}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\delta^{(2)}(z-z_i),
\end{equation}
which in its integral form is frequently called the Girko's formula (see a lucid discussion
e.g. in \cite{GKZ2011}). This or related formulas were of utmost importance for rigorously proving the famous Girko's circular law for the limiting mean density emerging as $N\to \infty$ and establishing its universality, see \cite{Bai97,TaoVu2008} and a review \cite{Bordenave-Chafai}.  Carefully working with the eigenvector of Hermitized Girko matrix Eq.(\ref{Hermitized})
corresponding to zero eigenvalue allows one to shed some light on corresponding eigenvectors of $X$, see e.g. \cite{Cipolloni_Erdos_Xu}.

In a version of this Hermitization approach one frequently defines the associated resolvent via
 \begin{equation}\label{HermitizedResolvent}
M_\epsilon(z,\overline{z})=\left(i\epsilon{\bf 1}_{2N}+Y_X(z,\overline{z})\right)^{-1}=\left(\begin{array}{cc} M^{(11)}_\epsilon(z,\overline{z}) &  M^{(12)}_\epsilon(z,\overline{z})\\  M^{(21)}_\epsilon(z,\overline{z})& M^{(22)}_\epsilon(z,\overline{z})\end{array}\right), \quad \epsilon>0.
\end{equation}
In terms of such a resolvent the expectation of the empirical density
 is readily represented, e.g. as $p_{N}(z)=\lim_{\epsilon{\to 0}}\left\langle\frac{1}{\pi }\partial_{z}\mbox{Tr} M^{(12)}_\epsilon(z,\overline{z})\right\rangle_X$.
 Insights into statistical properties of eigenvectors can be also achieved by carefully working with
Eq.(\ref{HermitizedResolvent}). In particular, the simplest object characterizing the eigenvector non-orthogonality,
the so-called Chalker-Mehlig \cite{ChalkerMehlig1998, MehligChalker2000} diagonal overlap defined as $ {\cal O}(z)=\left\langle \sum_{a=1}^N \mathcal{O}_{aa}\delta^{(2)}(z-z_a)\right\rangle_{X}$
can be accessed via the identity \cite{Janik1999}
\begin{equation}\label{Hermitized_Den_diag}  {\cal O}(z)=\lim_{\epsilon{\to 0}}\left\langle\frac{1}{\pi}\mbox{Tr} M^{(12)}_\epsilon(z,\overline{z})\mbox{Tr} M^{(21)}_\epsilon(z,\overline{z})\right\rangle_X
\end{equation}
or some equivalent relations.   The Hermitization resolvent approach remains one of principal available tools for eigenvector analysis, and has been recently utilized in some form in e.g. \cite{Cipolloni_Erdos_Xu}.

The main goal of the present paper is to develop an alternative approach for addressing statistics of both eigenvectors and eigenvectors of non-Hermitian
random matrices which is different from both methods outlined above, though the Hermitized matrix $Y_X(z,\overline{z})$ in fact also appears there in some form. The approach employs the framework of the so-called Kac-Rice counting formulas, see
\cite{Kac-Rice_lectures} for a detailed introduction into the subject, and the next section for implementation in the present context.
In that framework we prove the following

\begin{theorem} \label{MainMetaTheorem}
Consider a square $N\times N$ matrix  $X\in  \mathcal{M}_N\left(\C\right)$  with $N$ distinct eigenvalues $z_a,\,  a=1, \ldots, N$ and associated right eigenvectors ${\bf v}_a=(v_{1a},\ldots,v_{Na})^T$ assumed to be
 normalized  by  ${\bf v}_a^{*}{\bf v}_a=1$ and with the fixed phases $\theta_a=\theta$ for the first entry $v_{1a}$ of each eigenvector.
    Then the joint empirical density of an eigenvalue $z$ and the corresponding right eigenvector ${\bf v}$  with respect to the measure $d^2z\,d\mu_H({\bf v})$, further
  averaged over the phase $\theta$ uniformly distributed in $[0,2\pi)$
  is given by
\begin{equation}\label{Meta_Kac_Rice_right_eigenvec}
\Pi_N(z,{\bf v})=\frac{1}{\pi}\delta^{(2N)}\left(\left(X-z{\bf 1}\right){\bf v}\right)\,\left|\frac{d}{dz}\det\left(X-z{\bf 1}\right)\right|^2.
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

A proof of this Theorem can be found in the Appendix B.

In case of a random matrix $X$ the Theorem 1.1. should be complemented with the following

\begin{corollary}\label{main_corollary}
The ensuing JPD ${\cal P}_N(z,{\bf v})=\frac{1}{N}\left\langle\Pi_N\left(z,{\bf v}\right)\right\rangle_X$ of an eigenvalue $z$ and the associated normalized right eigenvector ${\bf v}$ for a random matrix $X$ can be represented as
\begin{equation}\label{main_practical}
{\cal P}_N(z,{\bf v})=\frac{1}{\pi N}\lim_{|w-z|\to 0}\frac{\partial^2}{\partial w\partial \overline{w}}\left\langle {\small
\left[\delta^{(2N)}\left(\left(z{\bf 1}-X\right){\bf v}\right)\,
\det{Y_X(w,\overline{w})}\right]}\right\rangle_{X},
\end{equation}
where $Y_X(z,\overline{z})$ is the Hermitized matrix defined in Eq.(\ref{Hermitized})
\end{corollary}

Such representation would however appear rather useless, unless one suggests an efficient way to evaluate the expectation of the right-hand side over a given ensemble of random matrices $X$. Fortunately, Eq.(\ref{main_practical}) does provide such a possibility. For this purpose one may employ the Fourier integral representation for the Gaussian-regularized Dirac delta-function factors,
see Eq.(\ref{Gaudelta}), namely in the following version: $\delta^{(2N)}\left(\left(X-z{\bf 1}\right){\bf v}\right)=\lim_{\epsilon\to 0}\delta^{(2N)}_{g,\epsilon}\left(\left(X-z{\bf 1}\right){\bf v}\right)$, where
\begin{equation}\label{Gaudelta_vec}
  \delta^{(2N)}_{g,\epsilon}\left(\left(X-z{\bf 1}\right){\bf v}\right)
=\int_{\mathbb{C}^N}e^{-\epsilon\frac{{\bf k}^*{\bf k}}{2}+\frac{i}{2}\left[{\bf k}^*\left(X-z{\bf 1}\right){\bf v}+
{\bf v}^*\left(X^*-\overline{z}{\bf 1}\right){\bf k}\right]}\frac{d{\bf k}d{\bf k}^*}{(2\pi)^{2N}}
\end{equation}
and we denote  $d{\bf k}d{\bf k}^*:=\prod_{i=1}^Nd \Re{k_i}d\Im{k_i}$ and ${\bf k}^*{\bf k}:=\sum_{i=1}^N\overline{k}_ik_i$ for ${\bf k}=(k_1,\ldots,k_N)^T\in \mathbb{C}^N$.
The Fourier integral representation of the $\delta-$function then can be combined with the Berezin Gaussian integral representation
 \begin{equation}\label{Berezin_int}
  \det{Y_X(w,\overline{w})}=(-1)^N\int \exp{\frac{i}{2}\left(\mathbf{\Psi}_1^T,\mathbf{\Psi}_2^T\right)
  \left(\begin{array}{cc}0 & X-w{\bf 1}\\ X^*-\overline{w}{\bf 1}& 0\end{array}\right)\left(\begin{array}{c}\mathbf{\Phi}_1 \\ \mathbf{\Phi}_2\end{array}\right)}\,{\cal D}(\mathbf{\Psi},\mathbf{\Phi})\, ,
  \end{equation}
  where $\mathbf{\Psi}_{\sigma}=\left(\psi_{\sigma,1},\ldots,\psi_{\sigma,N}\right)^T$ and $\mathbf{\Phi}_{\sigma}=\left(\phi_{\sigma,1},\ldots,\phi_{\sigma,N}\right)^T$ for $\sigma=1,2$
  are vectors with anticommuting/grassmann entries, and ${\cal D}(\mathbf{\Psi},\mathbf{\Phi})=\prod_{\sigma=1,2}\prod_{i=1}^N d\psi_{\sigma i} d\phi_{\sigma i}$.
  Employing such representations the expectation over $X$ in Eq.(\ref{main_practical}) can frequently be computed,  especially  straightforwardly if entries of $X$ are independent random Gaussian variables.
   In the main part of the paper we demonstrate viability of our approach by implementing such a strategy for two different choices of the ensemble for $X$, both cases being certain deformations of Ginibre ensembles.  In those cases we compute explicitly the associated JPD ${\cal P}_N(z,{\bf v})$ and eventually the mean eigenvalue densities  beyond currently available in the literature.

The organization of the paper is as follows.  A summary of the main results  is presented in the Section 2. It starts with a short survey of previous works
on eigenvectors of non-normal random matrices and related questions, putting our results in the appropriate context. Then we discuss the Kac-Rice counting formulas which provide the main framework for arriving at the statement of Theorem \ref{MainMetaTheorem}. The Section 2
 finishes with a discussion of open problems and a conjectured possible extension of the method allowing one to deal with the JPD of both right and left eigenvectors.
  Section 3  contains details of evaluation of  $\mathcal{P}_N(z,{\bf v})$ of the (normalized) right eigenvector and associated eigenvalue for an ensemble of random matrices interpolating between the real Ginibre and complex Ginibre ensembles.  Our approach yields exact and explicit formula for matrices of any size $N$, which together with  the associated  mean eigenvalue density (i.e. its marginal obtained after integrating over the eigenvector)  are then amenable to extracting the appropriate  scaling limits as $N\to \infty$.
In the next Section 4 we apply essentially the same method for evaluating $\mathcal{P}_N(z,{\bf v})$ on the matrices from the complex Ginibre ensemble $GinUE$ perturbed by a general fixed matrix $A$, possibly non-normal. The simplest particular case of rank-one perturbation is then discussed in greater detail.

Appendices contain technical details and proofs.

