\subsection{Appendix G: arguments supporting the conjecture \ref{conjecture}}

A natural starting point for arriving at the required joint empirical density is
to apply the Kac-Rice multivariate formula Eq.(\ref{Kac-Rice_counting_multi}) to the set of standard complex valued equations defining the right and left eigenvectors:
 \begin{equation} \label{start_left_right}
 X\,\mathbf{v}=z \mathbf{v}, \quad X^*\mathbf{u}= \overline{z}\mathbf{u}, \quad {\bf v}^*{\bf u}=1,
\end{equation}
written in terms of the associated real and imaginary parts of all relevant variables. One then can straightforwardly repeat all the steps presented in detail in the Appendix B and in this way show that
the associated Kac-Rice density for the complex triple $(z,\mathbf{v}, \mathbf{u})$ with respect to the flat Lebesque measure
 $d^2z\,d^{2N}\mathbf{u}\, d^{2N}\mathbf{v}$  acquires eventually the following form:
  \begin{equation} \label{unnorm_left_right}
 \Pi^{(KR)}(z,\mathbf{v}, \mathbf{u}) \propto
 \left|\det{\left(X-z{\bf 1}_N\right)}\right|^2\,\,\left|\frac{d}{dz}\det{\left(X-z{\bf 1}_N\right)}\right|^2
\end{equation}
\[\times \delta^{(2N)}\left( \left(X-z{\bf 1}_N\right)\mathbf{v}\right)\, \delta^{(2N)}\left(\left(X-z{\bf 1}_N\right)^*\mathbf{u}\right)\delta^{(2)}( {\bf v}^*{\bf u}-1).\]
One can however notice that the expression in the right-hand side can not {\it per se} give a proper probability density in the full space  $\mathbb{C}^{2N+2}$ as it is not normalizable with respect to the flat Lebesque measure. The reason is very simple: the conditions  Eq.(\ref{start_left_right})
determine the left-right eigenpair up to the "gauge invariance": $ \mathbf{v}\to c \mathbf{v}, \, \mathbf{u}\to c^{-1} \mathbf{u}$ for any complex factor $c\ne 0$,
and this residual gauge invariance is non-compact. The solution to the system Eq.(\ref{start_left_right}) is therefore
 given by a continuous family rather than a set of isolated points in  $\mathbb{C}^{2N+2}$
  making a naive application of the  Kac-Rice multivariate formula Eq.(\ref{Kac-Rice_counting_multi}) somewhat problematic. Nevertheless one may try to boldly convert  Eq.(\ref{unnorm_left_right}) to a probability density
simply by restricting the right eigenvector $\mathbf{v}$ to the unit complex $N-$sphere, and normalizing the resulting density against the product of the measure $d^2z\,d^{2N} \bf{u}$ with the standard Haar's measure on the complex sphere: $d\,\mu_H({\bf v})\propto \delta( {\bf v}^*{\bf v}-1)\,d^{2N}{\bf v} $. Such an {\it ad hoc} removal of the residual invariance looks most natural and forms the basis for putting forward the Conjecture \ref{conjecture}.

To check the compatibility of the proposed form Eq.(\ref{norm_left_right}) for the joint empirical density, one may wish to compare the marginal  $\tilde{\rho}_N(z) = \int_{\mathbf{S}^c_N} d\mu_H({\bf v}) \int_{\mathbb{C}^N}\Pi^{(KR)}(z,{\bf v},{\bf u})\,d^2{\bf u}\,d\mu_H({\bf v})$ with the empirical density $\rho_N(z)$ of complex eigenvalues given by the Kac-Rice formula for the
 matrix $X$ as given in Eq.(\ref{Kac-Rice_charpol}). The integration over the left eigenvector ${\bf u}$
 can be done using the following
\begin{proposition}
Consider a complex $N-$component vector ${\bf u}\in\mathbb{C}^N$. Then   the following distributional identity:
\begin{equation}\label{second_integral_identity}
\int_{\mathbb{C}^N} \delta^{(2N)} \left(B{\bf u}\right)\,\delta\left({\bf u}^*{\bf v}-1\right)\,d^{2N}{\bf u}=\frac{1}{\pi}\delta\left(|\det B|^2\right)=\delta^{(2)}\left(\det B\right)
\end{equation}
holds $\forall {\bf v}\in\mathbb{C}^N$ such that $|{\bf v}| \ne 0$.
\end{proposition}

The proof proceeds very similarly to the first proof of the identity Eq.(\ref{main_integral_identity_variant})
presented in the Appendix A and is omitted here.

Further employing Eq.(\ref{main_integral_identity}) one then completes the integration over the right eigenvector ${\bf v}$  over the complex sphere  yielding the marginal density in the form
\begin{equation}\label{Kac-Rice_charpol_new}
\tilde{\rho}_N(z)\propto
{\small \frac{1}{\pi}\left[\delta^{(2)}\left(P_N^{(char)}(z)\right)\right]^2\,\left|P_N^{(char)}(z)\right|^2\,
\left|\frac{d}{dz}P_N^{(char)}(z)\right|^2, \quad P_N^{(char)}(z):=\det{(X-z{\bf 1})}.}
\end{equation}
To correctly interpret the highly unusual squared delta-function factor in the right-hand side of the above equation  we claim that for any
polynomial  $P_N(z)$ with only simple roots in the complex plane holds the following distributional identity:
\begin{equation}\label{squared delta}
C\left[\delta^{(2)}\left(P_N(z)\right)\right]^2\,\left|P_N(z)\right|^2=\delta^{(2)}\left(P_N(z)\right).
\end{equation}
where $\delta-$function in both sides should be understood as the limit of an appropriate regularization, and the value of the constant $C$
explicitly depends on the chosen regularization. In particular, for the Gaussian-regularized $\delta-$function, understood as the limit of
\begin{equation}\label{Gaureg_comp}
\delta_{g,\epsilon}^{(2)}\left(P_N(z)\right)=\frac{1}{2\pi \epsilon}e^{-\frac{|P_N(z)|^2}{2\epsilon}},
\end{equation}
for $\epsilon \to 0$, the value of the constant is $C=4\pi$.


An argument in favour of the distributional relation Eq.(\ref{squared delta}) goes as follows. It is obviously enough to verify the identity in a small vicinity of a single root  of the polynomial $P_N(z)$ which we denote $z_0$.
Consider a disc $D_0\in \mathbb{C}$ of a radius $r_0>0$ centered at $z_0$.
Take the radius $r_0$ small enough as to (i) there is no any other roots of $P_N(z)$ inside $D_0$ and (ii) the polynomial inside $D_0$ can be
approximated as $P_N(z)\approx P'_N(z_0) (z-z_0)$. Now, take the $\epsilon-$regularized version of the left hand side of Eq.(\ref{squared delta})
and integrate it against a (good enough) test function $\phi(z,\overline{z})$, i.e. consider
 \begin{equation}\label{squared delta 1}
I^{(L)}_{\phi}(z_0,\epsilon)=C\int_{D_0}\left[\delta_{\epsilon}^{(2)}\left(P_N(z)\right)\right]^2\,\left|P_N(z)\right|^2\phi(z,\overline{z})dzd\overline{z}
\end{equation}
  Parametrizing $z=z_0+\sqrt{R}e^{i\theta}, \, 0\le R\le r^2_0$, and further using the linear approximation we see that
  \begin{equation}\label{squared delta 2}
I^{(L)}_{\phi}(z_0,\epsilon)\approx \frac{C}{2}\,\left|P_N(z_0)\right|^2\int_{0}^{2\pi}\int_0^{r_0^2}\frac{1}{(2\pi \epsilon)^2}e^{-\frac{|P_N(z_0)|^2R}{\epsilon}}\phi\left(z_0+\sqrt{R}e^{i\theta},\overline{z_0}+\sqrt{R}e^{-i\theta}\right) \,R\,dR
\end{equation}
Rescaling now $R\to \tilde{R}\frac{\epsilon}{|P'(z_0)|^2}$ one can easily evaluate the limit $\epsilon \to 0$ for a fixed $r_0$ finding
\begin{equation}\label{squared delta 3}
\lim_{\epsilon \to 0}I^{(L)}_{\phi}(z_0,\epsilon) = \frac{2\pi}{|P'(z_0)|^2} \frac{C}{2(2\pi)^2}\phi\left(z_0,\overline{z_0}\right)
\int_0^{\infty}e^{-\tilde{R}}\tilde{R}\,d\tilde{R}=\frac{C}{4\pi}\frac{\phi\left(z_0,\overline{z_0}\right)}{|P'(z_0)|^2}.
\end{equation}
On the other hand, repeating the same computation for
\begin{equation}\label{squared delta 4}
I^{(R)}_{\phi}(z_0,\epsilon)=\int_{D_0}\delta_{\epsilon}^{(2)}\left(P_N(z)\right)\,\phi(z,\overline{z})dzd\overline{z}
\end{equation}
one immediately gets
\begin{equation}\label{squared delta 3}
\lim_{\epsilon \to 0}I^{(R)}_{\phi}(z_0,\epsilon) = \frac{\phi\left(z_0,\overline{z_0}\right)}{|P'(z_0)|^2}.
\end{equation}
in full agreement by the identity Eq.(\ref{squared delta}).

With Eq.(\ref{squared delta})  in hand, the right-hand side of the expression Eq.(\ref{Kac-Rice_charpol_new}) becomes identical to
one in the right-hand side of Eq.(\ref{Kac-Rice_charpol}), verifying the consistency of the suggested form Eq.(\ref{unnorm_left_right}) for the  $\Pi_N(z,\mathbf{v},\mathbf{u})$, with ${\bf v}$ being confined to the unit complex $N-$sphere.

