\section{Introduction}

% \textcolor{red}{Precipitation is a fundamental component of the Earth's hydrological cycle, exerting a profound influence on ecosystems, food production, and economic systems~\cite{kotz2022effect}. Global precipitation products are typically derived from three main sources: in-situ gauge measurements, satellite-based remote sensing, and atmospheric reanalysis. Each source involves trade-offs among spatial coverage, temporal resolution, and retrieval accuracy, and no single product provides uniformly reliable estimates across all scales. Gauge-based datasets offer direct, high-quality measurements but suffer from sparse and uneven distribution, particularly over oceans, mountainous areas, and much of the Global South~\cite{tapiador2012global}. Satellite products such as IMERG~\cite{huffman2015nasa} provide near-global coverage and sub-hourly resolution but exhibit retrieval biases over multilayer cloud systems~\cite{haynes2022low} and complex terrain~\cite{qin2019cloud}. Reanalysis systems like ERA5~\cite{hersbach2020era5} ensure physical consistency, yet systematic errors remain due to parameterized microphysics (convections) and coarse resolution—commonly overestimating light precipitation~\cite{lazoglou2024multivariate, argueso2013precipitation, gutowski2003temporal} and underestimating extremes~\cite{borodina2017models}. Discrepancies in annual totals can exceed 300~mm across datasets~\cite{sun2018review}, highlighting the need for improved integration of observations, estimates, and model outputs.}


%Precipitation significantly impacts ecosystems, food security, and economic stability~\cite{kotz2022effect}. To accurately quantify precipitation, global precipitation records are typically derived from three primary sources: satellite-based retrievals, reanalysis, and in-situ gauge observations~\cite{sun2018review}. Each of these sources brings distinct advantages and limitations. Gauge networks provide accurate, direct measurements but are limited by sparse coverage, especially in less populated regions~\cite{tapiador2012global}. Satellite data, such as those from the GPM mission's IMERG~\cite{huffman2015nasa}, offer near-global coverage with high temporal resolution but face challenges in retrieval accuracy such as over multi-layer cloud structures~\cite{haynes2022low} and complex terrains~\cite{qin2019cloud}. Reanalysis products like ERA5~\cite{hersbach2020era5} provide globally consistent and physically constrained data, but are limited by model resolution and parameterized processes, leading to systematic biases such as overestimation of light rainfall~\cite{lazoglou2024multivariate, argueso2013precipitation, gutowski2003temporal} and underrepresentation of extreme events~\cite{borodina2017models}. Across these types, a persistent trade-off exists among coverage, resolution, and accuracy, with no single dataset offering a fully complete and unbiased representation across all spatiotemporal scales~\cite{sun2018review}.

