% Despite their promise, existing diffusion-based generative models impose structural constraints that limit their use for merging multi-modality precipitation records, including gridded products (e.g., satellite retrievals and reanalyses) and irregular gauge observations. These models typically require inputs to be fixed-shape tensors, implicitly assuming data lie on uniform spatial grids. Such architectural rigidity prevents the direct incorporation of sparse, non-gridded measurements—despite gauges often providing the most accurate and localized information. As a result, conventional generative models are ill-suited for fusing heterogeneous precipitation sources with differing spatial structures and uncertainties.To address this challenge, we propose PRIMER (\textbf{P}recipitation \textbf{R}ecord \textbf{I}nfinite \textbf{MER}ge), a novel generative framework that represents precipitation records as spatial functions, rather than as fixed-shape tensors defined on uniform grids with constant resolution. This formulation enables PRIMER to flexibly accommodate both uniformly gridded datasets (Fig~\ref{fig:Fig1}a, top) and sparse, irregular gauge observations (Fig~\ref{fig:Fig1}a, bottom) within a coherent representation. Once trained, PRIMER effectively merges information from multiple precipitation records into a unified prior distribution. This fusion is achieved through joint training on three complementary datasets—ERA5~\cite{hersbach2020era5}, IMERG~\cite{huffman2015nasa} and gauge observations~\cite{shen2014high}. This generative merging effectively “jail-breaks” the traditional constraints imposed by single-source or grid-constrained approaches. PRIMER supports a broad range of downstream applications, including bias correction and super-resolution through principled posterior sampling. Empirical evaluations demonstrate that datasets refined by PRIMER consistently outperform raw existing products, as validated against independent gauge observations from 2016. Across more than 1,000 gauge stations, approximately 85\% exhibit reduced errors in both mean absolute error (MAE), reflecting deterministic accuracy, and continuous ranked probability score (CRPS), reflecting probabilistic forecast skill. Beyond site-level improvements, PRIMER enhances climatological fidelity by more accurately capturing high-impact rainfall events, increasing spatial details at mesoscale wavelengths, and more faithfully reproducing the observed spatial organization of precipitation systems. Notably, PRIMER also generalizes to new operational forecasts, correcting biases in numerical weather prediction outputs without the need for additional training.

