\paragraph{{Color Singlet Retrieval and the End State}}
In usual QCD processes, asymptotic color-neutral hadrons eventually form. In a black hole, any color “hair” is naively hidden behind the horizon. However, QMM memory for color flux implies that evaporating partons can retrieve color data in correlated jets, albeit in a Planck-scale or redshifted context. When the black hole nears Planckian mass, the gravitational imprint sector merges with QCD imprint states~\cite{rovelli1996black, neukart2024quantum}. The entire process stays unitary: the black hole does not disappear leaving hidden color microstates, since QMM degrees of freedom release color or flavor data back to the external field.

