\section{Introduction}

Modern next-generation sequencing (NGS) platforms generate massive numbers of short subsequences of DNA referred to as \textit{reads}, which consist of sequences of nucleotide bases \(A, T, C\) and \(G\) \cite{Metzker2010}. % but lacking information about where they came from in the genome. %The data we consider in this work are such short sequences of nucleotide bases \(A,T,C\) and \(G\). 
Reads contain no inherent positional information. Therefore, reconstructing the full genome of an individual from reads requires reassembling them, which is typically done by aligning them to the most similar region in a reference genome -- a process known as \textit{read mapping} or \textit{read alignment }\citep{Alser2021}. Performing a unique read alignment proves challenging when there are repetitive or highly similar segments in the genome. When reads are shorter, ambiguities become more prevalent \citep{Logsdon2020}.
One of the leading short read sequencing platforms, Illumina, produces reads that can vary from 30 to 500 base pairs (bp) \citep{Goodwin2016}, but recent developments in DNA sequencing have made long read sequencing more reliable and available, producing reads of lengths 10k to 100k bp \cite{Marx2023}. This greatly facilitates alignment, as most regions are unique at those length scales.
In this work, we analyze sequences 30bp in length, which would represent read lengths that can occur in ancient DNA (aDNA) sequencing \cite{Bettisworth2025-ug}. aDNA is characterized by specific damage patterns, such as cytosine deamination that increases individual nucleotide errors, and also suffers from severe fragmentation \cite{briggs}. 
Fragmentation of genetic material prevents the recovery of longer reads, regardless of advancements in sequencing technology.
Thus, robust methods for analyzing ultra-short read data are still needed for us to learn about our past through studying genetics.


%If the reads contain longer sequences than the 30 bp our model is trained for, they can still be analyzed by drawing subsequences from the read and processing those.
%In this work, we focus on genetic data, specifically short sequences of DNA known as \emph{short reads}, which typically range from  30 to 500 base pairs (bp) in length.  
%Modern sequencing technologies generate an enormous number of these reads, but without information about where they came from in the genome. To reconstruct an individuals full genome these reads must be reassembled. This is usually done by finding the best match to a reference genome -- a process known as read mapping or read alignment.
%When sequencing an individual with next generation sequencing, the genomic material is fragmented, and the sequencer returns a massive number of short snippets which has to be pieced back together to represent the full genome.
