This means that a read can be expressed in terms of its \textit{reverse complement}, i.e., in reversed order and with the bases changed as \(A \leftrightarrow T\) and \( C \leftrightarrow G \) when compared to the reference genome, which describes just one of the strands.
Figure \ref{fig:reads} illustrates an excerpt of the double helix, and shows that reads from the same genomic position can be expressed differently depending on which strand the read is taken from. To handle real short-read data, the model must recognize a \(k\)-mer and its reverse complement as similar.
