$\bullet$ CMB: In our analysis, we incorporate the complete set of Planck 2018 measurements \citep{2020A&A...641A...6P,2020A&A...641A...5P,2020A&A...641A...1P}. This includes the temperature anisotropy power spectrum (TT),  polarization spectra (EE and TE), their cross-correlations, and the reconstructed lensing potential power spectrum, and provides significantly improved constraints compared to previous releases - the inclusion of high-resolution polarization data reduces parameter uncertainties by approximately 40\% relative to the temperature-only (TT+lowP) analysis. This comprehensive CMB dataset serves as a crucial anchor for our cosmological parameter estimation. The combined dataset, which we refer to as \textbf{``CMB"}.



We implement the theoretical model in a modified version of the \textsf{CLASS} Boltzmann solver code \citep{2011JCAP...07..034B} and employ the publicly available \textsf{Cobaya}$\footnote{\url{https://github.com/CobayaSampler/cobaya}}$ sampling framework \citep{2021JCAP...05..057T} for Bayesian parameter estimation through Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) analysis. In our analysis, we incorporate this code and combine it with the likelihoods from DESI BAO and SN Ia to study our target cosmological models.
To ensure the reliability of MCMC sampling, we implement the Gelman-Rubin convergence diagnostic criterion \cite{1992StaSc...7..457G}, enforcing a strict convergence threshold ($R-1<0.01$) across all models and datasets. For parameter estimation, we adopt uniform priors on the cosmological parameter set
$\{\Omega_bh^2, \Omega_ch^2,\tau_{reio},\theta_s,\log(10A_s),n_s,\alpha\}$ and perform statistical analysis of posterior distributions using the \textsf{GetDist} package$\footnote{\url{https://github.com/cmbant/getdist}}$, obtaining key statistical measures including one-dimensional marginalized distributions and two-dimensional joint confidence regions. Bayesian evidence $\log\mathcal{Z}$ is calculated through the MCEvidence$\footnote{\url{https://github.com/yabebalFantaye/MCEvidence}}$ code \citep{2017arXiv170403472H,2017PhRvL.119j1301H}.

\section{Results and Discussion}\label{sec:4}

\begin{figure*}
\begin{center}
{\includegraphics[width=1.0\linewidth]{cmbresults.pdf}}
\end{center}
\caption{The one and two-dimensional marginalised posterior distributions in a torsion cosmology from the different datasets.}  \label{torsion1}
\end{figure*}

Our analysis of the torsion cosmology model, combining the DESI, PantheonPlus/DESY5 and CMB datasets, yields robust constraints on key cosmological parameters.  The 1D marginalized posterior distributions and 2D joint confidence regions displays in Fig. \ref{torsion1} and numerical results of the constraints are shown in Table  \ref{tab1}.

The CMB-alone analysis produces relatively weak constraints on the torsion parameter $\alpha = 0.042^{+0.035}_{-0.031}$ and Hubble constant $H_0 = 60.7 \pm 5.2~\mathrm{km~s^{-1}~Mpc^{-1}}$. The large uncertainties reflect the well-known geometric degeneracy inherent in CMB data. Notably, the matter density $\Omega_m = 0.397^{+0.048}_{-0.087}$ shows a $>2\sigma$ tension with late-time measurements, while the high $\chi^2_{\rm min} = 2826.71$ indicates poor fit quality, demonstrating that CMB data alone cannot effectively constrain torsion cosmology. Adding DESY5 data, we have that the CMB+DESY5 combination significantly tightens constraints, the uncertainty of $\alpha$ reduces by $\sim75\%$ to $0.0109\pm0.0085$ and the precision of $H_0$ improves five times to $65.7\pm1.1\ \mathrm{km\ s^{-1}\ Mpc^{-1}}$, and $\Omega_m$ becomes consistent with other probes ($0.332\pm0.012$). Using DESI+DESY5+CMB delivers transformative improvements:
$\alpha$ shifts negative ($-0.0041\pm0.0057$) at $1.7\sigma$ significance within zero value, $H_0$ precision reaches $0.7\%$ ($68.48\pm0.47$), and  $\Omega_m$ and $\sigma_8$ uncertainties shrink by $\sim65\%$. The $\Delta\chi^2\approx-26$ relative to DESY5+CMB demonstrates BAO data's power to break degeneracies, particularly through the $D_M(z)/r_d$ and $H(z)r_d$ measurements.
Combination of full dataset, i.e., DESI+PantheonPlus+CMB provides the most robust constraints: $\alpha = -0.0075\pm0.0058$ (consistent with $\Lambda$CDM at $1.3\sigma$), $H_0 = 68.80\pm0.48\ \mathrm{km\ s^{-1}\ Mpc^{-1}}$ (resolving Hubble tension), and $\sigma_8 = 0.8134\pm0.0055$ (alleviating $\sigma_8$ tension).


The findings in the torsion cosmology model reveals several key findings. First, the torsion parameter $\alpha$ shows a clear evolution across datasets, from $\alpha =0.042^{+0.035}_{-0.031}$ (CMB alone) to $\alpha=-0.0075\pm0.0058$ (DESI+PantheonPlus+CMB). While consistent with zero within $1\sigma$, the negative central value in the full dataset combination suggests a possible weak torsion effect in the late universe. The Hubble constant converges to $H_0 = 68.48\pm0.47\ \mathrm{km\ s^{-1}\ Mpc^{-1}}$ (DESI+DESY5+CMB), effectively bridging early and late-universe measurements and alleviating the Hubble tension.

Second, the matter sector parameters demonstrate excellent consistency with $\Lambda$CDM expectations. We find $\Omega_m =0.3026\pm0.0039$ and $\sigma_8=0.8124\pm0.0056$ for the DESI+DESY5+CMB combination, showing no significant tension with Planck measurements. The primordial parameters remain stable across datasets, with $n_s =0.9688\pm0.0039$ and $\log(10^{10}A_s)=3.053\pm0.014$, maintaining agreement with CMB constraints while incorporating low-redshift data. The torsion-modified growth equation, $\delta''_m + 2\mathcal{H}(1-\alpha a)\delta'_m - 4\pi Ga^2{\rho}_m\delta_m = 0$, predicts $\sigma_8 = 0.8124 \pm 0.0056$, relieving the CMB-weak lensing tension through suppressed structure growth. This framework yields exceptional concordance with KiDS-1000 ($S_8=\sigma_8\sqrt{\Omega_m/0.3}=0.815^{+0.016}_{-0.021}$) \citep{2025arXiv250319441W}, reducing its Planck tension from $0.73\sigma$ in $\Lambda$CDM to just $0.1\sigma$. Notably, the torsion term $\alpha a$ introduces scale-dependent damping, naturally lowering $\sigma_8$ without disrupting CMB constraints—a unique advantage over $\Lambda$CDM extensions.

Finally, the model comparison statistics demonstrate that the torsion framework provides excellent fits to all datasets, as evidenced by the favorable $\Delta\chi^{2}_{\rm min}$ values relative to $\Lambda$CDM. Based on the Bayesian evidence $\Delta\log\mathcal{Z}$, all datasets except CMB+DESI+DESY5 show varying degrees of preference for torsion model. Notably, the CMB+DESI+DESY5 combination exhibits a slight preference for $\Lambda$CDM with $\Delta\log\mathcal{Z} = 3.01$.
Interestingly, when replacing DESY5 with PantheonPlus supernova data, this result reverses dramatically, yielding $\Delta\log\mathcal{Z} = -3.59$ and $\Delta\chi^{2}_{\rm min} = -2.30$ - an unexpected outcome. This suggests that different supernova datasets can significantly alter the model comparison results.
The evolution of $\Delta\log\mathcal{Z}$ values across datasets - particularly the supernovae-driven transition from negative to positive evidence - highlights the importance of combining multiple probes when testing modified gravity theories. While current data doesn't conclusively favor torsion cosmology ($|\Delta\log\mathcal{Z}| < 5$ in all cases), its consistent performance across model comparison metrics and ability to resolve cosmological tensions make it a compelling extension to $\Lambda$CDM worthy of further investigation.




\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:5}

Our analysis of torsion cosmology reveals several interesting features worth noting. The torsion parameter $\alpha$ shows a gradual evolution from $\alpha = 0.042^{+0.035}_{-0.031}$ in the early universe to $\alpha = -0.0075\pm0.0058$ in the late universe, though remaining consistent with zero within $1\sigma$ uncertainties. This pattern could suggest a redshift-dependent behavior of torsion effects that may become more noticeable at lower redshifts.

The framework shows some promise in addressing cosmological tensions, with the Hubble constant converging to $H_0 = 68.80\pm0.48~\mathrm{km~s^{-1}~Mpc^{-1}}$ and the $S_8$ tension reducing from $0.73\sigma$ to $0.1\sigma$ when compared with KiDS-1000 data. The torsion model introduces scale-dependent damping, naturally lowering $\sigma_8$ without disrupting CMB constraints—a unique advantage over $\Lambda$CDM extensions.

The torsion framework provides excellent fits to all datasets, as evidenced by the favorable $\Delta\chi^{2}_{\rm min}$ values relative to $\Lambda$CDM. The Bayesian evidence analysis presents mixed results, with $\Delta\log\mathcal{Z}$ values ranging from $-3.59$ to $+3.01$ depending on the dataset combination. The positive $\Delta\log\mathcal{Z} = +3.01$ for the DESI+DESY5+CMB combination is particularly noteworthy, while the other cases show a slight preference for torsion cosmology. This variation suggests the model's performance may be sensitive to different observational probes.

Future data from ongoing surveys could help clarify these findings. The framework's predictions for BAO measurements and the determine of $\alpha$ may merit further investigation to better understand its implications for cosmic acceleration and structure formation. The current results, while not conclusive, suggest torsion cosmology warrants consideration as a potential extension to the standard cosmological model.

